(Bug #66: a top-level declaration is global wherever it sits - LANGUAGE.md's
 "Variables declared at top level are global and can be used inside
 functions" attaches no ordering condition, and the analyzer already accepts
 the name. Codegen learned a global's type only when the walk reached the
 declaration, so a function generated above it read the slot as a raw
 machine word: a text printed its rodata address, a float its IEEE-754 bits,
 a list or a buffer a live heap address. Every read below is of a global
 declared AFTER the function that reads it, and must answer exactly what the
 same read at top level answers.)

To 'show all'.
    Print counter.
    Print label.
    Print ratio.
    Print items.
    Print payload.
    Print scores.
    Print flagged.

a number called counter is 42.
a text called label is "hello".
a float called ratio is 2.5.
a list called items is [1, 2, 3].
a buffer called payload is "ABC".
a map called scores is {"a": 1}.
a boolean called flagged is yes.

'show all'.

(The same reads at top level, where the declaration is already in scope -
 the two columns of the register's table, and they must agree.)
Print counter.
Print label.
Print ratio.
Print items.
Print payload.
Print scores.
Print flagged.
